+# Send pickup reminders by SMS
+
+For whoever proposes changing the pickup reminder channel to save money. Pickup reminders go by SMS because the
+email trial saw more missed pickups and wasted fresh food. This page owns the reason for keeping SMS.
+
+## Context
+
+[Issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md) records the latest proposal to replace SMS reminders with free email to save
+40 euros a month. It was the third time volunteers had asked that year.
+
+The pantry tried email reminders from April to June 2025. According to the provider's report cited in the issue,
+34% of reminder emails were never opened. Missed pickups rose from 6% to 13% of pantry orders. A missed pickup
+leaves a packed bag behind, and its fresh food goes in the bin by closing time.
+
+The pantry lead reported that many members share one phone in the family and never check email, but do see SMS.
+The pantry switched back to SMS on July 1, 2025; missed pickups were back to 6% by August.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email**: free to send, but the trial saw more missed pickups and wasted fresh food; many members do not check it.
+- **SMS**: costs 40 euros a month as reported in issue 52, but reaches members who do not check email; chosen.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep pickup reminders on SMS, as the maintainer confirmed when closing issue 52. Reaching members in time for
+pickup and avoiding wasted fresh food outweigh the saving from switching to email.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The pantry continues paying for SMS reminders. Free email alone is not a reason to switch: any future proposal
+needs to address members' access to reminders and the missed pickups observed in the email trial.
